      "costUsd": 0.0083265,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with this question. Tear one page at random off this desk calendar, and every page has the same chance. But is every date, from the first to the thirty-first, equally likely? Make a guess. Let's flip through a whole year and count the pages for each date. January has every date from one to thirty-one. February stops at twenty-eight. By the end of the year, the dates one to twenty-eight have twelve pages each, the twenty-ninth and thirtieth have eleven, and the thirty-first has only seven. Every page is equally likely, but the date isn't. So when is a distribution uniform, and how do we check?"
    },
