      "inputTokens": 149,
      "outputTokens": 1239,
      "costUsd": 0.0112255,
      "pronunciationText": "A discrete random variable is uniformly distributed if it has finitely many values, all with the same probability. Uniform just means even, like a flat row of bars. And the shared probability isn't a free choice. Say there are n values, each with probability c. They must add to one, so n times c is one, and c is one over n. The page itself is uniform: three hundred and sixty-five pages, each with probability one over three hundred and sixty-five. A fair die has six values, so each is one sixth. A random digit from zero to nine has ten values, so each is one tenth. So when something is chosen with equal chances from a finite list, a uniform model fits."
    },
