      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 132,
      "outputTokens": 889,
      "costUsd": 0.008067,
      "pronunciationText": "So why isn't the date uniform? Look behind each bar. The first of the month sits on twelve equally likely pages, one in every month. The thirty-first sits on just seven, because February, April, June, September and November are too short. So P of the first is twelve over three hundred and sixty-five, and P of the thirty-first is only seven over three hundred and sixty-five. Here's the test. Uniform is a property of the random variable, not of the equipment. Ask: does every value come from the same number of equally likely outcomes? For the date, the answer is no."
    },
