      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 129,
      "outputTokens": 1008,
      "costUsd": 0.0091365,
      "pronunciationText": "The classic case is two fair dice, added. Make one die white and one red, so three then four is a different outcome from four then three. That gives thirty-six outcomes, and with independent fair dice they're all equally likely. If you only list the twenty-one unordered pairs, they aren't equally likely, because a double happens one way and a mixed pair two ways. A sum of twelve needs six and six, just one outcome. A sum of seven runs along this whole diagonal, six outcomes. So seven is six times as likely as twelve. The outcomes are uniform, but the sum is not."
    },
